      "content": "Babies are active learners from birth. They learn through their senses—seeing, hearing, touching, tasting, smelling, and moving.\n\n\nBabies learn when adults:\n\n- Talk, sing, and make eye contact\n- Respond to cries, smiles, gestures, and sounds\n- Allow safe touching and exploration\n- Repeat familiar songs, names, and routines\n- Play simple games such as peek-a-boo\n- Name everyday objects and actions\n\n\n> **Responsive caregiving:** WHO calls this responsive caregiving: noticing, understanding, and responding to a child’s signals in a timely and appropriate way. Responsive care supports health, nutrition, safety, learning, and emotional security.\n\n\n#### Everyday examples\n\n- During feeding, a mother looks into her baby’s eyes, smiles, and says, “You are drinking your milk.”\n- A father shakes a plastic bottle filled with dry beans, and the baby listens, watches, and reaches for it.\n- A grandmother repeatedly calls the baby’s name during bathing, and the baby begins to turn toward the familiar voice.\n- A baby drops a spoon again and again. The caregiver calmly says, “The spoon fell down,” then gives it back. The baby is learning about falling, sound, and repetition.\n\n\n> **Clinical Evidence:** CDC guidance notes that babies learn through play and interaction, including talking, reading, singing, peek-a-boo, and safe exploration.",
