      "title": "4. Cognitive Development From 0–5",
      "order": 4,
      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "> **Important Guidance:** Children develop cognitive skills at different rates. The patterns below are general guides, not deadlines or examinations.\n\n\n### Age Stage: 0–12 months\n\nBabies learn mainly through their senses, movement, and relationships.\n\n**Common learning experiences include:**\n\n- Looking at faces and following moving objects\n- Recognizing familiar people and voices\n- Reaching for and grasping objects\n- Putting safe objects in the mouth to explore them\n- Learning cause and effect, such as shaking a rattle to make a sound\n- Looking for a partly hidden toy\n- Enjoying repetition and simple games\n\nBy around one year, many children explore objects by shaking, banging, throwing, putting things into containers, and taking them out. They may also copy gestures and follow simple directions.\n\n\n\n### Age Stage: 1–2 years\n\nToddlers become curious explorers and begin to solve simple problems.\n\n**Common learning experiences include:**\n\n- Imitating adults and older children\n- Understanding what familiar objects are used for\n- Following simple instructions\n- Finding hidden objects\n- Sorting objects by simple features\n- Beginning pretend play, such as feeding a doll\n- Testing how things work\n\nAt around 18 months, many children know what ordinary objects are for, such as a spoon, brush, or telephone, and begin simple pretend play.\n\n\n\n### Age Stage: 2–3 years\n\nChildren’s memory, language, and imagination grow quickly.\n\n**Common learning experiences include:**\n\n- Asking many questions\n- Sorting shapes and colours\n- Matching objects\n- Building towers\n- Completing simple puzzles\n- Following two-step instructions\n- Playing simple pretend games\n- Understanding ideas such as “in,” “on,” and “under”\n\nBy age two, many children can find objects hidden under two or three covers, begin sorting shapes and colours, and play simple make-believe games.\n\n\n\n### Age Stage: 3–5 years\n\nPreschool children become more thoughtful, imaginative, and independent.\n\n**Common learning experiences include:**\n\n- Asking “why” questions\n- Remembering parts of stories\n- Counting and beginning to understand numbers\n- Understanding “same” and “different”\n- Following rules in games\n- Planning simple activities\n- Playing more complex pretend games\n- Solving simple problems with growing independence\n\nBy age four, many children understand the idea of counting, remember parts of a story, and understand “same” and “different.” By age five, many can count objects, draw a person with several body parts, and tell a simple story.",
      "coachNotes": "Guide the learner through cognitive development from 0–5, highlighting cognitive milestones, responsive caregiving, language interaction, and safe problem solving."
