      "title": "5. Attention and Memory",
      "order": 5,
      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "> **Attention:** Attention means the ability to focus on something.\n\n> **Memory:** Memory means the ability to keep and recall information.\n\n\nYoung children naturally have short attention spans. This is normal. A toddler may focus on a puzzle for a short time, then move to another activity. This does not mean the child is lazy or badly behaved.\n\n\nAttention improves gradually as children grow, especially when activities are interesting, playful, and suited to their age.\n\n\n### What helps attention and memory?\n\n- **Repetition:** Singing the same song, reading the same story, or repeating a child’s name helps learning.\n- **Routines:** Regular meal, bath, play, and sleep routines help children predict what comes next.\n- **Conversation:** Talking about what happened during the day helps children remember events.\n- **Songs and rhymes:** Rhythm and repetition make information easier to remember.\n- **Stories:** Stories build listening, memory, imagination, and language.\n- **Hands-on play:** Children remember more when they can touch, move, build, sort, and explore.\n\n**Example:** A caregiver can sing the same simple hand-washing song each day. Over time, the child begins to remember the steps: wet hands, use soap, rub hands, rinse.\n\n\n\n\n> **Important Guidance:** Do not expect every child to concentrate for the same length of time. Follow the child’s interest and keep learning activities short, enjoyable, and practical.",
