      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "Young children ask questions, touch things, open containers, take objects apart, and repeat actions because they are learning.\n\n\n> **Curiosity:** Curiosity is the desire to know, see, try, or understand something.\n\n> **Exploration:** Exploration is the child’s active effort to investigate the world.\n\n\nA child who opens every cupboard is not necessarily being naughty. The child may be learning:\n\n- What is inside\n- How doors open and close\n- What objects look like\n- What happens when something falls\n- What adults allow and what adults stop\n\n\nSafe exploration is essential for learning. WHO emphasizes that early learning happens when children use their senses, move, hear and use language, experience different places, interact with people, and explore objects.\n\n\n### Encouraging safe curiosity\n\n> **Safety Guidance:** Keep dangerous items out of reach: medicines, chemicals, sharp objects, matches, small objects that can cause choking, and hot items.\n\n- Create a safe play area with safe household objects.\n- Allow the child to touch, stack, pour, sort, and investigate.\n- Answer questions simply and honestly.\n- Turn questions into learning moments.\n\n**Example:** If a child asks, “Why does water spill?” the caregiver can say, “Water moves and flows. Let us pour a little water from this cup into the bowl and watch.”",
