      "order": 7,
      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "> **Problem-solving:** Problem-solving means finding a way to reach a goal or deal with a challenge.\n\n\nChildren learn to solve problems gradually. They may:\n\n- Try something\n- Observe what happens\n- Try again\n- Change their approach\n- Ask for help\n- Find a solution\n\n\n#### Everyday examples\n\n- A baby reaches for a toy that is slightly out of reach and rolls closer to it.\n- A toddler tries to fit a round object into a square hole, then tries another hole.\n- A preschool child builds a tower, watches it fall, and tries a wider base the next time.\n- A child looks for a missing shoe under the bed, behind a chair, and inside a basket.\n\n\nAdults should not solve every problem immediately. Give the child a little time to try. Offer encouragement and small hints rather than taking over.\n\n\n**Example:** Instead of saying, “Give it to me, I will do it,” a caregiver can say, “Try turning the lid this way. You are working hard. Do you want help now?”\n\n\n\nThis builds confidence, patience, persistence, and thinking skills.",
