      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "> **Play:** Play is one of the most important ways young children learn. WHO defines play as voluntary, enjoyable activity that may be imaginative, social, or solitary, and may involve objects. Young children build and strengthen developmental skills through playful interactions with people and objects.\n\n\n### Types of play\n\n#### Types of play\n\n| Type of Play | What It Supports | Low-Cost / Everyday Materials |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Building and construction** | Planning, problem-solving, hand control | Blocks, plastic cups, empty cartons, small stones |\n| **Pretend play** | Imagination, memory, language, understanding daily life | Feeding a doll, cooking with empty pots, pretending to go to market |\n| **Sorting and matching** | Thinking, comparing, early mathematics | Sorting bottle caps by colour, matching socks, grouping leaves |\n| **Puzzles** | Attention, problem-solving, visual thinking | Homemade picture puzzles from old cards or magazines |\n| **Drawing** | Creativity, memory, hand control | Paper, charcoal, pencil, crayons, sand drawing |\n| **Storytelling** | Language, memory, imagination | Family stories, picture books, local folktales |\n| **Songs and rhymes** | Memory, attention, rhythm, language | Local songs, counting songs, action rhymes |\n| **Outdoor exploration** | Observation, curiosity, problem-solving | Looking at leaves, insects, clouds, shapes, and sounds |\n\nChildren do not need expensive toys. Safe household objects and homemade materials can provide rich learning opportunities. WHO/UNICEF guidance encourages caregivers to use common household objects and homemade toys, along with talking, singing, and book sharing.",
