      "order": 9,
      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "Talking with children is one of the most powerful ways to support cognitive development. Language gives children words for objects, feelings, actions, and ideas.\n\n\nAdults can support thinking by:\n\n- Talking during daily activities\n- Naming objects and actions\n- Asking simple questions\n- Listening to the child’s answers\n- Reading or telling stories\n- Singing songs and rhymes\n- Describing what is happening\n- Encouraging children to explain their ideas\n\n\n**Example:** While cooking, a caregiver can say, “I am washing the tomatoes. The tomato is red. Now I am cutting it. What colour is this one?”\n\n\n\nThis does not require special materials or a formal classroom. Everyday activities such as feeding, bathing, walking, cooking, farming, shopping, and travelling can become learning moments. WHO notes that early learning opportunities are present in daily life, including at home, childcare, worship places, health facilities, and markets.",
