      "content": "A child’s environment strongly influences learning opportunities. This does not mean a family needs wealth or many toys. It means creating safe, warm, responsive, and stimulating daily experiences.\n\n\nHelpful elements include:\n\n- **Safe exploration:** A clean, safe space where the child can move, touch, and play.\n- **Responsive adults:** Adults who notice and respond to the child’s sounds, questions, and interests.\n- **Books and stories:** Reading, picture sharing, storytelling, and local stories.\n- **Conversation:** Talking with the child in the family language.\n- **Play opportunities:** Time for free play, pretend play, and active play.\n- **Predictable routines:** Regular meals, sleep, hygiene, and play routines.\n- **Sleep:** Enough sleep supports attention, memory, mood, and learning.\n- **Nutrition:** Good nutrition supports brain development, energy, and concentration.\n- **Physical activity:** Movement supports overall development and helps children learn through the body.\n- **Reduced unnecessary screen exposure:** Screens should not replace play, conversation, sleep, or physical activity.\n- **Emotional security:** A child who feels safe and loved is more able to explore and learn.\n\n\n### Nurturing Care Framework (WHO)\n\n**Core Components:**\n- Good health\n- Adequate nutrition\n- Responsive caregiving\n- Opportunities for early learning\n- Safety and security\n\n\n> **Clinical Evidence:** WHO’s Nurturing Care Framework identifies good health, adequate nutrition, responsive caregiving, opportunities for early learning, and safety and security as essential conditions for children to thrive.\n\n\n> **Key Principle:** Families with limited resources can still support learning through talking, singing, storytelling, household play materials, routines, affection, and safe exploration.",
