      "order": 11,
      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "Screens are part of modern life, but they should not replace the real-world experiences that young children need most: play, movement, conversation, relationships, and exploration.\n\n\n> **Clinical Evidence:** The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends avoiding screen media other than video-chatting for children younger than 18 months. For children aged 18–24 months, if families choose to introduce media, it should be high-quality and used together with an adult. For children aged 2–5 years, the AAP recommends limiting screen use to one hour or less per day of high-quality programming, with adult involvement.\n\n\n#### AAP screen media recommendations by age\n\n| Age Range | Recommendation |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Under 18 months** | Avoid screen media other than video-chatting |\n| **18–24 months** | If families choose to introduce media, it should be high-quality and used together with an adult |\n| **2–5 years** | One hour or less per day of high-quality programming, with adult involvement |\n\n\n### Practical guidance\n\n- Choose calm, age-appropriate, high-quality content.\n- Watch or use screens with the child when possible.\n- Talk about what the child is seeing.\n- Avoid screens during meals and for about one hour before bedtime.\n- Keep bedrooms screen-free.\n- Do not use screens as the main way to calm a child.\n- Make sure screens do not replace sleep, active play, reading, or family interaction.\n\n\n\nScreens are not automatically harmful, but passive or excessive use can reduce time for the interactions and activities that best support learning. The AAP emphasizes that young children learn best through hands-on exploration and social interaction with trusted caregivers.",
