      "title": "13. Recognizing Possible Concerns",
      "order": 13,
      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "> **Important Guidance:** A developmental concern does not automatically mean something is wrong. One behaviour alone does not diagnose a condition.\n\n\nSpeak with a doctor, nurse, or other qualified child-health or developmental professional if you notice:\n\n- The child is not making expected progress in learning, attention, or problem-solving over time.\n- The child has significant difficulty understanding simple instructions or participating in age-appropriate play.\n- The child does not respond to their name, familiar people, or everyday sounds.\n- The child has persistent difficulty paying attention, remembering, or learning through play.\n- The child loses skills they previously had.\n- The child seems unusually disconnected from people, objects, or play.\n- The parent or caregiver has a concern that continues or seems unusual.\n\n\n> **Clinical Evidence:** CDC advises caregivers to act early if a child is missing milestones, has lost previously acquired skills, or if the caregiver has any concern, and to ask for developmental screening.\n\n\n> **Developmental screening:** Developmental screening is a short, structured check used by trained professionals to decide whether a child may need a fuller assessment. It is not a diagnosis.",
