      "title": "14. African Family Case Study",
      "order": 14,
      "hasVideo": false,
      "content": "### Case Study: Case: Ngozi and her three-year-old daughter, Chiamaka\n\n**Setting:** Enugu, Nigeria\n**Participants:** Ngozi (Parent), Chiamaka (Child, 3 years)\n\nNgozi lives in Enugu, Nigeria, with her three-year-old daughter, Chiamaka. Chiamaka asks many questions: “Why is the pot hot?” “Where does rain come from?” “Why does the fan turn?”\n\nShe also opens plastic containers, takes lids off bottles, empties baskets, and repeatedly tries to fit objects into other objects. Ngozi sometimes feels Chiamaka is being troublesome, especially when she is busy cooking.\n\nOne afternoon, Ngozi decides to observe Chiamaka more closely. She notices that Chiamaka is not simply making a mess. She is:\n\n- Trying different lids on different containers\n- Sorting bottle caps by colour\n- Asking what will happen when she pours water from one cup to another\n- Pretending to cook after watching Ngozi in the kitchen\n- Remembering the steps of a song she learned at church\n\nNgozi creates a safer learning space. She gives Chiamaka clean plastic containers, safe lids, cups, and a bowl of dry beans. She sits nearby and asks, “Which lid fits this container? What do you think will happen if we pour the beans into the cup?”\n\nChiamaka tries, spills a few beans, laughs, and tries again. Ngozi says, “You are finding the right lid. Well done for trying again.”\n\nNgozi still keeps dangerous items locked away and supervises closely. She also answers Chiamaka’s questions in simple words and encourages her to explain her ideas.\n\n#### What this case teaches:\n\n- Curiosity and exploration are important parts of learning.\n- A child’s “messy” behaviour can be an opportunity for problem-solving.\n- Adults can turn everyday household objects into learning materials.\n- Questions should be welcomed, not discouraged.\n- Safety and supervision remain essential.\n\n#### Discussion questions and model answers:\n\n1. **Why might Chiamaka keep opening containers and fitting objects together?**\n   *Model Guidance:* She may be exploring size, shape, cause and effect, and problem-solving. She is learning through repetition and hands-on play.\n\n2. **How did Ngozi turn a challenging behaviour into a learning opportunity?**\n   *Model Guidance:* She observed Chiamaka, provided safe materials, asked open questions, and encouraged her to try again without shaming her.\n\n3. **What cognitive skills was Chiamaka practising?**\n   *Model Guidance:* She was practising problem-solving, sorting, matching, memory, curiosity, language, and pretend play.\n\n4. **What should Ngozi do to keep the activity safe?**\n   *Model Guidance:* She should supervise closely, remove choking hazards and sharp or harmful objects, and keep medicines, chemicals, and hot items out of reach.\n\n5. **When should Ngozi seek professional advice?**\n   *Model Guidance:* If Chiamaka’s concerns persist, if she stops making progress, or if she loses skills she previously had, Ngozi should discuss this with a qualified health or developmental professional.",
